":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"correct\",\"rationale\":\"The answer covers the central causes in the key points. Portfolio causes include sampling/optimization, incomplete or delayed index rebalances, cash drag and flows, and constraints leaving the portfolio off-index, which approximates diversification limits. Pricing causes include stale or asynchronous prices, fair value, and benchmark methodology or data mismatches. Operating causes include fee accruals, holdings or cash errors, and processing and settlement issues. Consistent treatment of distributions and corporate actions approximates dividend-accrual timing, though accrual timing is not named explicitly. It correctly separates cumulative tracking difference (total return gap) from tracking error (variability of daily active returns). It implicitly addresses measurement-method alignment by calling for same-date returns and consistent fee, distribution and benchmark conventions. It does not explicitly say a constant fee shifts the mean rather than raising the standard deviation, but it makes no contrary error. Fees appear only as accrual or operational issues. Minor omissions: it does not explicitly require agreeing the benchmark and period up front, and does not name variable expenses as such. Neither is material.\"}","ttft_ms":2522,"type":"result","duration_ms":6096,"uuid":"9533d359-c92d-4f3b-a2aa-903e574e5e44","ttft_stream_ms":497,"time_to_request_ms":59,"first_content_frame_ms":497,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
